    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d692">

                <pb facs="0915" n="903" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg903"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.24596/8–2746: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Philippines</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McNutt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Manila</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-08-27T09:00:00+08:00">August 27, 1946—9
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 27—1:35 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>246. Mytel 242, August 26.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d692-f1"/> Although language
                    some parts Department’s draft military base agreement suggest mere executive <pb
                        facs="0916" n="904" xml:id="pg904"/> agreement intended, other sections
                    indicate formal treaty. At any rate President Philippines considers he cannot
                    commit his government without consent Philippine Senate. This being so, presume
                    US Senate must also advise consent ratification. If this presumption correct,
                    please instruct regarding rewording article 29 your draft. If not, please
                    explain how ratification unnecessary our end for communication to Philippine
                        Government.<anchor n="72" xml:id="d692-f2"/></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">McNutt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d692-f1" n="71" xml:id="d692-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d692-f2" n="72" xml:id="d692-f2-content">
                <p>In telegram 310, September 6, 6 p.m., to Manila, the Department replied that it
                    contemplated an executive agreement, which the President had been authorized to
                    conclude in joint resolution approved June 29, 1944 (Public Law 380, 79th
                    Congress). The Department added, “If Roxas decides ratification Phil Cong
                    necessary, you are authorized delete ‘signature’ in Art 29 and insert
                    ‘acceptance by both Governments’.” (811.24596/8–2746)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
